On Sat, 14 Aug 2004 14:44:14 UTC, psmithnews@spod-central.org (Dylan 
O'Donnell) wrote:

> die@spammer.die (Mark Healey) writes:
> > OK, I've figured out how to do it within mapglyph().  Now the problem
> > is that it isn't called for "you" unless you move.  Where is it
> > determined that the hero glyph needs to be redrawn?
>
> In domove():
>
>   if(u.ux0 != u.ux || u.uy0 != u.uy) {
>       u.umoved = TRUE;
>       /* Clean old position -- vision_recalc() will print our new one. */
>       newsym(u.ux0,u.uy0);
>       /* Since the hero has moved, adjust what can be seen/unseen. */
>       vision_recalc(1);   /* Do the work now in the recover time. */
>
> The glyph gets redrawn at various other times, of course, for example
> if you polymorph (the newsym(u.ux,u.uy) call in polymon()). You'll
> either want to have a newsym() call for your location every time
> something happens that could change the colour under your scheme, or
> (probably simpler though less efficient or accurate) each time round
> the action loop.

That sounds like an esthetic choice.  It looks like I might do
